  "query_preview": "..\n\nWind and rain are the main focus now through Friday evening as the \nstrong low pressure system riding up the east coast is expansive \nenough to reach west and impact the Great Lakes as well. The edge of \nthe precip shield has arrived on track this afternoon across far \nsouthern MI and will expand northward through the rest of the day as \nthe low drifts up the coast. Initially, the surface low pressure \ncenter is located near the NC/VA coastal border, but there is a \ntrough extending westward through TN which resides under the strong \nupper low and is expected to become the more dominate surface low \nheading into Friday. This low then tracks northeast through Ohio and \nalong the St Lawrence Seaway Friday through Saturday morning. \n\nSo where does this leave SE MI in terms of wind and rain? The \ninitial Fgen and deformation tied to the current low is lifting into \nSE MI this afternoon and is quiet strong due to the overall strength \nof the system. And though we are quiet a distance west of the warm \nconveyor belt, a 60 knot easterly jet will be more than adequate to \ndraw moisture into the region. The peak of the rain looks to come \nthis evening when the strongest frontal forcing coincides with the \nenhances moisture transport. PWATs will peak around 1 inch before \nsettling back down around 0.75 inch for most of tonight into Friday. \nThere is a dry slot as seen on current regional radar mosaic that \nwill slide up later tonight leading to period of lighter rain toward \nmorning. Then the stacked low will bring additional moisture into \nthe area and then it pulls the deformation band back east through \nthe area during the day keeping showers going. Overall the QPF \namounts look to range from 0.75 inch west of I75 up to 1.25 inches \nalong the eastern CWA. \n\nIn terms of precipitation type, we are expecting primarily rain, \nespecially in the heavier rain band this evening due to the warm \nnose extending up to around 700mb. Winds do turn slightly more \nnortherly after midnight which does attempt to cool the column with \nan isothermal layer to around 800mb and then nearly isothermal up \nthrough 600mb. As the column gets cold enough, the moisture actually \ngets stripped out of the layer colder than -10C, thus drizzle or \nlight rain would still be the preferred ptype. Additionally, surface \ndewpoints are forecast to hold in the mid 30s tonight which won't \nhelp any flakes survive the fall. Though we could see some snow \nflakes mix in tonight with the slightly cooler air, the cold air \nadvection doesn't look strong enough to convert the precip column to \nenough flakes to result in accumulation. \n\nEasterly winds have been elevated today with gusts to around 20 \nknots, but will increase this evening toward 30 knots as the strong \neasterly jet flares up. Winds turn more northerly Friday and though \nthe gradient remains fairly tight, the heights will be increasing \nthrough the day there will be a lull in winds aloft so we are only \nable to mix down winds to around 20 knots. Still will make for an \nunpleasant day with highs only near 40F, the wind chill values will \nlinger around 32F with light rain most of the day. \n\nShortwave ridge slides over the region Friday night bringing an end \nto the rain and providing for a quiet Saturday with highs in the mid \n40s. Elevated warm front lifts through the region Saturday afternoon \nbut there's alot of dry air below 600mb to contend with so looks \nlike mostly clouds will accompany the developing front. Attention \nthen turns to the next trough rotating through the Plains and up \ninto the Great Lakes on Sunday, bringing the next chance of \nwidespread rain to the region.",
